Material deposition onto implicit surfaces, via "fix ablate" with its
mode = deposit setting: corner point values are incremented instead of
decremented, so the surface grows into the gas instead of receding.

These inputs are the regression-testable core of the deposition
verification.  Each one's stats columns encode an invariant, so the
reference logs in this directory enforce it on every run:

1) in.deposit.conserve grows a surface in a sealed reflective box.
   With no inflow or outflow, the drop in particle count must equal
   the buried-particle counter exactly at every output step: nothing
   is ever lost silently.  The rate is high enough that particles ARE
   buried, and "fix grid/check 1 error" aborts the run if any particle
   is ever left inside the grown surface.

2) in.deposit.rate drives a flat front with a rate given in
   length/time (units = distance) and outputs the front speed the
   surface actually realized, which must equal the requested rate.
   This is the analytic-rate calibration: a plane normal to x on a
   graded corner point field moves at exactly the speed asked for.

3) in.ablate.inert runs the same sealed box in the default ablate
   mode.  The deposition counters must read zero throughout, which
   pins down that the advancing-front machinery is fully inert when
   deposition is not in use.

All three also output the requested-vs-applied accounting pair
(f_ablate[1] and f_ablate[22]): the corner point budget the source
asked for, and the change the field actually took, whose difference is
the amount discarded at saturated corner points.

A much larger verification suite lives in tools/testing/deposition:
momentum and energy conservation ledgers closed to round-off in fully
periodic boxes, per-species sticking against free-molecular closed
forms, oblique-front rates measured from dumped corner values outside
SPARTA, cross-processor burial independence, load balancing under a
growing film, and negative tests for the guards.  Run it with

  cd tools/testing/deposition
  python3 run_tests.py                 # serial
  python3 run_tests.py --exe ../../../src/spa_mpi --ranks 4

See the "fix ablate" doc page for the deposition keywords (mode, units,
response, density, sticking) and the discussion of what the advancing
front does to particles in its way.
